":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:3-1--case:cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.md:occ-f2952f7c5cdd","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.html","repository_path":"cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":104},"excerpt":"piece of the power Christ has given to His Church. Thus BCO 3-1 states “The","sources":["case_markdown_text","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.md"},{"id":"bco:3-1--case:cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.md:occ-2e7f297f1141","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.html","repository_path":"cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":121},"excerpt":"…church.\" The word “all\" in 6-4 is critical. Given the principles set forth in BCO 3-1 and 16-1,2 it is unreasonable to think that the word all in 6-4 is somehow meant to exclude some communicants from the right to vote in congregational elections unless…","sources":["case_markdown_text","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.md"},{"id":"bco:3-1--case:cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.md:occ-ea23fdb1093a","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.html","repository_path":"cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":614},"excerpt":"Given the principles set forth in BCO 3-1 and 16-1,2 it is unreasonable to think that the word \"all\" in 6-4 is somehow meant to exclude some communicants from the right to vote in congregational elections unless there is a clear provision somewhere else in…","sources":["case_markdown_text","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga51_2024__2023-11.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["minor communicant voting","congregational meeting","voting rights","good and regular standing","church bylaws","officer election","communicant membership","BCO 6-4","BCO 20-3","BCO 24-3","BCO 25-1","age restrictions","session authority"],"standard_of_review":"not_stated","case_numbers":["2023-11"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"The SJC sustained Psiaki’s complaint and held that the CPC Session erred by ordaining and installing officers elected at a February 5, 2023 congregational meeting where the congregation’s bylaw excluded communing members under 18 from voting. Reading BCO 6-4, 25-1, and 25-7 together, the SJC ruled that all communing members are entitled to vote and that local bylaws cannot impose an age restriction; the “good and regular standing” language addresses censure, not age. The decision did not annul the completed ordination/installation, but required future elections and installations to comply with the Constitution."}},{"id":"bco:3-1--case:cases/ga30_2002__2001-07.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga30_2002__2001-07.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Andy Lee v. Korean Capital Presbytery","year":2002,"disposition":"dismissed","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga30_2002__2001-07.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga30_2002__2001-07.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:3-1--case:cases/ga30_2002__2001-07.md:occ-da330241a774","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga30_2002__2001-07.html","repository_path":"cases/ga30_2002__2001-07.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":92},"excerpt":"Furthermore, BCO 3-1 underscores the right of the congregation to be governed by those duly elected by that body.","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga30_2002__2001-07.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["complaint","original jurisdiction","Session","administrative commission","congregational consent"],"standard_of_review":"not_stated","case_numbers":["2001-7"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"A complaint challenged Presbytery’s suspension of a church Session and installation of a commission to perform Session functions without prior congregational consent. The SJC sustained the complaint and annulled all subsequent commission actions as the Session insofar as the commission was improperly constituted. Presbytery could not suspend elected officers and replace them without prior consent; later recognition could not cure the defect. The SJC also rejected Presbytery’s objection that a related appeal barred the complaint because the cases presented fundamentally different issues."}},{"id":"bco:3-1--case:cases/ga19_1991__1990-04.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga19_1991__1990-04.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Chappell v. Eastern Carolina Presbytery","year":1991,"disposition":"sustained","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga19_1991__1990-04.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga19_1991__1990-04.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:3-1--case:cases/ga19_1991__1990-04.md:occ-f33c1d49ee35","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga19_1991__1990-04.html","repository_path":"cases/ga19_1991__1990-04.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":513},"excerpt":"3. BCO 3-1 The Power which Christ has committed to His Church rests in the whole body, the rulers and the ruled, constituting it a spiritual commonwealth. This power, as exercised by the people, extends to the choice of those officers whom He has appointed in…","sources":["case_markdown_text","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga19_1991__1990-04.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["presbytery commission powers","Session Pro Tem","suspension of session without due process","limited jurisdiction of church courts","ministerial and declarative power","congregational right to choose officers","ecclesiastical versus civil power","Fuller Memorial Presbyterian Church","delegation of sessional duties","complaint","ruling elder","presbytery action"],"standard_of_review":"not_stated","case_numbers":["1990-4"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"Ruling Elder Chappell complained against Eastern Carolina Presbytery's Commission, which had suspended the session of Fuller Memorial Presbyterian Church and constituted itself as a 'Session Pro Tem' without due process or congregational consent. The SJC sustained the complaint, holding that the BCO grants no authority for a presbytery to depose a session and act in its place, that suspension requires due process, and that the congregation has an inalienable right to be governed by officers it has chosen."}},{"id":"bco:3-1--case:cases/ga19_1991__1990-04a.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga19_1991__1990-04a.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Marshall v. Eastern Carolina Presbytery","year":1991,"disposition":"sustained","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga19_1991__1990-04a.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga19_1991__1990-04a.md","evidence_basis":"structured_case_metadata","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:3-1--case:cases/ga19_1991__1990-04a.md:occ-9cf6b47f3000","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga19_1991__1990-04a.html","repository_path":"cases/ga19_1991__1990-04a.md","locator":{},"excerpt":"","sources":["cases.jsonl:bco_cited_as","cases.jsonl:bco_cited_current","judicial_cases.jsonl:bco_provisions"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga19_1991__1990-04a.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["presbytery commission powers","Session Pro Tem","suspension of session without due process","limited jurisdiction of church courts","ministerial and declarative power","congregational right to choose officers","Fuller Memorial Presbyterian Church","teaching elder complaint"],"standard_of_review":"not_stated","case_numbers":["1990-4a"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"Teaching Elder Wallace Marshall complained against the same Eastern Carolina Presbytery actions at Fuller Memorial Presbyterian Church that were the subject of Case 90-4, filed separately as a Teaching Elder complainant. The SJC sustained this complaint on essentially the same grounds as Case 90-4, finding that the presbytery lacked authority to suspend the session without due process and to constitute itself as a 'Session Pro Tem.'"}},{"id":"bco:3-1--overture:ga12_1984:14","record_id":"overture:ga12_1984:14","type":"Overture","title":"Adopt Statement Encouraging Memorial Services for Miscarried Infants","year":1984,"disposition":"Adopted (final)","authority_weight":"medium","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/markdown/ga12_1984.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/markdown/ga12_1984.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"bco:3-1--overture:ga12_1984:14:occ-01e09c4819bb","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/markdown/ga12_1984.html#ga12-p73","repository_path":"markdown/ga12_1984.md#ga12-p73","locator":{"fragment":"ga12-p73","page":73},"excerpt":"…requires ,h